# Owning a language-server session

A language server is an independent program. Once started, it has a process, open
documents, pending requests, timers, input and output streams, and its own idea of
the workspace. Treating it as a stateless function would hide the hardest part of
the integration: deciding who owns all of that state and when it must stop being
trusted.

Literate owns the process directly. A session belongs to one local workspace folder
and one canonical programming-language identity. It starts on demand, receives
projected source through standard input and output, and remains alive for later
requests. A JavaScript- or TypeScript-family session receives all projected outputs
in that family so imports between them can be understood together. The session is
still keyed by the selected canonical language; other language identities remain
separate.

The extension does not borrow another extension's private process, install a server,
or discover an executable from project files. Executable configuration is read from
global user settings. The command must be an absolute path, arguments must be plain
strings, and the process is launched directly without a shell.

## Starting a server is a trust decision

A configured server is ordinary executable code with the access of the extension
host. Projection protects generated files from being written, but it does not
sandbox the server. The server can still read workspace files such as imported
libraries and project configuration.

For that reason Literate starts sessions only for trusted, local, file-backed
workspace folders and open local `.literate` documents interpreted as Markdown.
Remote extension hosts, non-file documents, and untrusted workspaces do not cross
this boundary. The restriction is checked again around asynchronous work because
trust, folder membership, and document state can change while a process is starting.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Absent
    Absent --> Initializing: eligible request or background sync
    Initializing --> Ready: handshake and capabilities accepted
    Initializing --> Failed: startup or protocol failure
    Ready --> Ready: synchronize and request
    Ready --> Failed: transport or protocol failure
    Ready --> Stopping: reset or disposal
    Stopping --> Absent: process ended
    Failed --> Absent: explicit reset
```

The arrows describe ownership transitions, not every protocol message. The key
feature is the terminal `Failed` state. A broken session does not enter an automatic
restart loop; it remains unavailable until configuration changes or the user asks
Literate to restart its language servers.

## The handshake establishes a shared language

Initialization tells the server which workspace the session represents and which
client capabilities Literate is prepared to honor. Literate advertises a deliberately
small surface: document synchronization, hover, completion, signature help,
navigation, diagnostics, and constrained code actions.

The handshake also fixes the position encoding. Literate offers UTF-16 and rejects a
server that explicitly chooses another encoding. This is a semantic requirement,
not a preference: all projection ranges, editor positions, and protocol positions
must count columns in the same units.

Concurrent callers share one initialization attempt. They do not race to launch
duplicate processes. A caller may cancel its own wait without cancelling the shared
handshake needed by another operation.

## Real URIs, virtual contents

The server sees each projection at the `file:` URI where its generated output would
normally live. This gives imports and project configuration realistic context. The
text associated with that URI comes from the captured projection, however, whether
or not a file exists there and whether or not a saved generated file is current.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Editor
    participant Owner as Literate session owner
    participant Server as Language server
    participant Disk

    Editor->>Owner: Captured project snapshot
    Owner->>Owner: Build projections in memory
    Owner->>Server: Open or change intended output URIs
    Server->>Disk: Read physical imports and project configuration as needed
    Owner->>Server: Request at a projected position
    Server-->>Owner: Reply in generated-file coordinates
    Owner-->>Editor: Validated mapped result
```

The server-to-disk arrow is important: the configured process remains a trusted
workspace program. The absence of an owner-to-disk generation arrow is equally
important. Synchronizing projected documents does not save source and does not
materialize generated outputs.

## Synchronization is a complete document-set decision

The session owner supplies the complete current set of projected documents for that
language. New documents are opened, changed documents are updated, removed documents
are closed, and unchanged text sends no notification. A change of language identity
closes and reopens the URI rather than pretending that only its text changed.

Each changed URI receives a monotonically increasing wire revision. Reopening a URI
does not reuse an earlier number. This makes later diagnostics correlatable with the
specific text the server saw.

Projected documents require a server that supports opening and closing documents
and accepts full document text. If a server advertises incremental changes, Literate
still sends one replacement covering the complete previous text. The replacement
range is measured in UTF-16, including the correct width of the final line. This
keeps synchronization simple while remaining valid for both full-text and
incremental servers.

The whole document set is validated before notifications are sent. Duplicate URIs,
unsupported synchronization, excessive document counts, or excessive serialized
input fail explicitly. They do not leave a silently truncated project context.

`language client sync` validates the whole document set before sending any
notification.

```ts : <<language client sync>>=
    public async sync(documents: LanguageDocument[]): Promise<void> {
        await this.initialize();
        if (this.closing || this.stopped) { throw this.stopped || new Error('Language client disposed'); }
        const sync = this.capabilities.textDocumentSync;
        const kind = typeof sync === 'number' ? sync : object(sync) ? sync.change : 0;
        if (documents.length && kind !== 1 && kind !== 2) { throw new Error('Language server document synchronization unsupported'); }
        const openClose = typeof sync === 'number' || (object(sync) && sync.openClose === true);
        if (documents.length && !openClose) { throw new Error('Language server open/close synchronization required for projections'); }
        const next = new Map(documents.map(doc => [doc.uri, { ...doc }]));
        let bytes = 0;
        for (const doc of next.values()) { bytes += Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(doc), 'utf8'); }
        if (documents.length > 1024 || next.size !== documents.length || bytes > limit / 2) {
            throw new Error('LSP document set limit exceeded or duplicate URI');
        }
        for (const [uri, old] of this.documents) {
            if (!next.has(uri) || next.get(uri)!.languageId !== old.languageId) {
                this.documents.delete(uri);
                if (openClose) { this.send({ method: 'textDocument/didClose', params: { textDocument: { uri } } }); }
            }
        }
        for (const doc of next.values()) { this.updateDocument(doc, kind, openClose); }
    }

```

## The protocol is an untrusted byte stream

Language Server Protocol messages are JSON values framed by byte lengths. A character
count cannot safely stand in for a byte count because UTF-8 uses different numbers
of bytes for different characters. Literate therefore parses headers and bodies as
bytes, validates the declared length, decodes complete bodies strictly, and only
then examines JSON.

Incoming headers, message bodies, queued output, pending requests, listeners, and
the synchronized document set all have fixed limits. Requests have deadlines.
Initialization has its own longer deadline. These bounds prevent a stalled or
hostile process from retaining unlimited memory or keeping an editor operation alive
forever.

Cancellation has two stages. If work is cancelled before a request is sent, no
protocol request is created. If it is already on the wire, the local promise is
rejected and one cancellation notification is sent to the server. A response that
arrives later no longer owns a pending request and cannot revive the cancelled work.

## Server requests are answered narrowly

A language server can send requests back to its client. Literate answers only the
small set needed by this integration: configured settings, the owning workspace
folder, and diagnostic refresh. A request asking the client to apply a workspace
edit is refused. Unknown methods receive an unsupported-method response rather than
being interpreted dynamically.

This is part of the read boundary. The transport carries server data, but it does
not turn arbitrary server messages into editor commands, filesystem operations, or
new protocol registrations. Responses are still validated by the feature adapter
before they become editor objects.

## Failure has one cleanup path

Malformed framing, invalid protocol envelopes, a closed stream, process exit, or an
unrecoverable write failure stops the session. Pending requests are rejected,
document revisions and buffered input are cleared, listeners are released, streams
are destroyed, and the child process is terminated if necessary. Stop observers run
once, after document state has been cleared.

Normal disposal first offers the server a bounded cooperative shutdown, then sends
its exit notification, and finally forces termination if the process does not end.
Repeated disposal calls share the same shutdown rather than racing several cleanup
sequences.

Owning this lifecycle gives the rest of the editor a stable promise: a request uses
one initialized process and one synchronized document set, or it produces no result.
The next chapter follows the safe, read-only answers that can cross back from that
session into a literate document.
